OBELS et al. 2020 -- inter-rater reliability on the outcome, unpacked
==============================================================================
Reported percentages are the INITIAL coding, before the scheme was refined
and disagreements adjudicated. The published 21/36 is post-adjudication.

what                       agree%      n     k of n 95% CI on agr. kappa (equal marginals)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SPSS  executability           60%     20      12/20   0.36 - 0.81    INFEAS
SPSS  reproducibility         55%     20      11/20   0.32 - 0.77    +0.07
R     executability           75%     16      12/16   0.48 - 0.93    +0.30
R     reproducibility         56%     16       9/16   0.30 - 0.80    +0.10

KAPPA IS NOT IDENTIFIED FROM PERCENTAGE AGREEMENT. Range over all marginal
pairs (grid of 0.05) consistent with each reported agreement:

  SPSS  executability      kappa in [-0.25, +0.31]   (equal marginals at the base rate: INFEASIBLE)
  SPSS  reproducibility    kappa in [-0.29, +0.25]   (equal marginals at the base rate: +0.07)
  R     executability      kappa in [-0.14, +0.53]   (equal marginals at the base rate: +0.30)
  R     reproducibility    kappa in [-0.25, +0.25]   (equal marginals at the base rate: +0.10)

READ IT THIS WAY. For the two EXECUTABILITY rows the equal-marginal table is
INFEASIBLE -- with ~88% of scripts called executable by each coder, chance
agreement alone would exceed the agreement actually observed. The coders'
marginals therefore differed substantially, which is consistent with the
paper saying their expertise did. For the two REPRODUCIBILITY rows the
equal-marginal kappa is 0.07 and 0.10 and the feasible range straddles zero.

So this is NOT evidence that kappa was 0.1. It is evidence that:

  (a) percentage agreement on this outcome was 55-56% on a base rate near
      58%, i.e. close to what two independent coders drawing at the base
      rate would achieve by construction;
  (b) the interval on that percentage, at n = 13 and 17, is enormous;
  (c) NOBODY ELSE IN THIS LITERATURE MEASURED IT AT ALL.

(c) is the finding. A field that reports reproduction rates to three
significant figures has exactly one measurement of whether two people
looking at the same artifact call it the same thing, and it is 55%, on
seventeen papers, and it was reported as an aside about coder training.
